cartridge "SLOTS/Roger Sliva/1986"

' SLOTS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Slots", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, August 1986
' (Commodore 64 / Plus/4 BASIC with a 528-byte machine-language computer
' player at $309D, disk 1986-08; no author named in the listing).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy
' by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from the
' BASIC listing, orig/slots.bas, and the disassembly, orig/ml.dis),
' rebuilt for the ColecoVision: falling piece sprites, a rack of tiles,
' sound effects, a title tune and a match.
'
' From the original:
' - you against the computer on a rack of 8 slots (columns), 8 pieces
'   deep; a piece drops to the lowest free place of the slot you choose;
' - you win with four of your pieces in a line (across, up-down or
'   diagonal) or by putting a piece in the TOP row (the 8th place of a
'   slot); the computer wins the same ways. The top-row rule means a
'   full rack can never happen (the 57th piece at the latest is in the
'   top row), so there is no draw; the port still checks for a full rack;
' - you choose who goes first; the computer's opening move is random,
'   slot INT(RND * 7 + .2) + 1 (slot 8 only 3 times in 100);
' - level 1-4 = how many moves the computer looks ahead (its own, yours,
'   its own, yours); the original changed it with L during your move;
' - the computer (orig/ml.dis, ported exactly, quirks included): for each
'   slot in turn it scores the place a piece would land: 64 + place / 8
'   (place = row * 9 + slot), reset to 0 first in row 7 (it would hand
'   the top row to the other side); +2, +8, +36 for the 1st, 2nd, 3rd
'   neighbouring enemy piece along each line through the place (a
'   block), +1, +2, +16 for its own; three of its own in a line = a win,
'   played at once; a top-row place is played at once too; if the
'   place above would give the enemy three in a line, the score drops to
'   0; deeper levels add the best reply's score halved, quartered, ...
'   (as the ML does: the reply's best score ADDED, and the best scores of
'   deeper levels carried over between sibling moves); each score gets a
'   random 0-3 (the C64's raster line AND 3) and the highest wins, the
'   later slot on a tie.
'
' Liberties: joystick: left/right moves the piece over the slots, the
' button drops it (the C64 typed 1-8); "go first" and the level are a
' menu before each game (L on the C64); a match: first to 3 games, with
' the score shown; the top row is marked red and "TOP ROW OR 4 IN A LINE
' WINS" is shown (the C64 did not explain its top-row rule on screen);
' the computer's random 0-3 comes from a 16-bit xorshift instead of the
' raster line; the computer's thinking time is the Z80's (level 4 takes a
' few seconds, as it did on the C64).
'
' Controls: controller 1: left/right choose a slot, a button drops the
' piece. Menus: up/down choose a line, left/right change it, a button
' picks.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' screens drawn with the NMI off after a wait, display off. Music first
' in the sound table, effects after; the title tune is played once.
' `set sprite count` is always given.

